order: 3
description: Key log paths — auth, daemon, kern, cron, Xorg
tags: [logs, log-files, var-log, auth, kern, xorg]
---

Key log file locations on Artix Linux. Without journald, all logs are traditional text files in /var/log/.

@text
Core System Logs

@code bash
# General system messages (most important catch-all)
/var/log/messages

# Authentication: sudo, login, ssh, su
/var/log/auth.log

# Kernel messages (same content as dmesg, but persistent)
/var/log/kern.log

# Daemon/service messages
/var/log/daemon.log

# Cron job output and scheduling
/var/log/cron.log

# Mail system (if running an MTA)
/var/log/mail.log

@text
Application Logs

@code bash
# X.Org / Xwayland display server
/var/log/Xorg.0.log           # current session
/var/log/Xorg.0.log.old       # previous session

# Package manager
/var/log/pacman.log            # all pacman transactions (install/remove/upgrade)

# Boot log (if boot logging enabled)
/var/log/boot.log

@text
Quick Viewing Commands

@code bash
# View recent auth events
tail -50 /var/log/auth.log

# Follow system messages live
tail -f /var/log/messages

# Follow multiple logs simultaneously
tail -f /var/log/messages /var/log/auth.log /var/log/kern.log

# Use multitail for side-by-side log viewing
multitail /var/log/messages /var/log/auth.log

@text
Searching Logs

@code bash
# Find SSH login attempts
grep "sshd" /var/log/auth.log
grep "Failed password" /var/log/auth.log
grep "Accepted" /var/log/auth.log

# Find sudo usage
grep "sudo" /var/log/auth.log

# Find kernel errors
grep -i "error\|fail\|panic" /var/log/kern.log

# Find cron job executions
grep "CRON" /var/log/cron.log

# Search with timestamp range (assuming syslog format)
awk '/May 10 14:00/,/May 10 15:00/' /var/log/messages

# Find recent package operations
tail -50 /var/log/pacman.log
grep "installed\|removed\|upgraded" /var/log/pacman.log | tail -20

# Find Xorg errors
grep -iE "EE|error|fatal" /var/log/Xorg.0.log

@text
Log Permissions and Ownership

@code bash
# View log file permissions
ls -la /var/log/

# Typical permissions:
# -rw-r-----  root  log   /var/log/messages
# -rw-r-----  root  log   /var/log/auth.log
# -rw-r-----  root  log   /var/log/kern.log
# -rw-r--r--  root  root  /var/log/pacman.log
# -rw-r--r--  root  root  /var/log/Xorg.0.log

# Most logs require root or 'log' group membership to read
# Add user to log group (if needed)
sudo usermod -aG log $USER

@text
Log Disk Usage

@code bash
# Check log directory size
du -sh /var/log/
du -sh /var/log/* | sort -h

# Find largest log files
find /var/log -type f -exec du -h {} + | sort -rh | head -20

# Clean old compressed logs manually
sudo rm /var/log/*.gz
sudo rm /var/log/old/*
